more people are living in slavery today  than at any other time in history  today is the world day against human  trafficking designed to raise awareness  of the problem  it's a one aspect of modern day slavery  but there the united nations estimates  more than 40 million people worldwide  are trapped in some form of slavery  women and girls make up around 70  percent of the victims  many are exploited for sexual slavery  while the problem is said to be most  prevalent in africa and asia  there's also an estimated 1.5 million  slaves in the developed world  police here in germany recently made  arrests linked to a suspected  international smuggling ring  although the vast majority of people who  are trafficked are women and young girls  men are trafficked as well we have this  report from pakistan about a man who was  kidnapped and forced to work  in quarries for years before escaping  his captors he's still coming to terms  with the time he spent  as a slave  this television and video recorder are  the only luxuries muhammad arif has  he lives in a small nine square meter  simple hut  in northern pakistan arif once had a  family  and a job and was happy but then as a  young adult  he was kidnapped by three men  they put something over my head and i  lost consciousness  when i regained my senses i had no idea  where i was  they brutalized me over and over again i  remember that  to this day  they held him captive for more than 20  years  he worked up to 16 hours a day in a  quarry like this one  breaks were not allowed anyone who sat  down was beaten by the guards  but he remembers that the nights were  even worse than the days  at night they would lock us up and put  us in chains  they threw food at us that you wouldn't  even give to dogs  it was barbaric that's how i spent my  life there with no contact to the  outside world  arif was also unable to contact his wife  shamim bibi  she spent several years desperately  trying to find her husband  eventually she gave up thinking he was  dead and so  she looked for a new husband  we searched everywhere for him called  all the hospitals  but at some point we had no strength  left it had been 10 years  the children were still small and i  needed someone to take care of them  according to un figures between 600 and  800  000 people are victims of trafficking  every year  in pakistan officially documented cases  have declined in recent years  but the crimes still continue to this  day  in pakistan this happens mostly in  remote areas  where there is hardly any infrastructure  and a lack of education  the perpetrators then kidnap people and  receive a reward  most of the times the victims are put to  work  as fate would have it muhammad arif is  still hauling stones  and manages to scrape by doing this work  at least he's no longer doing it as a  slave laborer  because one morning he decided to jump  off the boat that took him and the other  men to work  i jumped into the water and started  swimming then they shot at me  but i was lucky and they didn't hit me  for hours i kept swimming at some point  i dared to get out of the water  it was night but i made it to the next  village where they helped me  a few years ago he came back to his home  village of haripur  he still suffers from the trauma of that  time  to this day he has not found a way to  regain his life  for more on this story we can speak now  to siobhan malali in galway ireland  she is the un special rapporteur on  human trafficking  welcome to dw we just saw there in our  report the incredible story  of a survivor of human trafficking but  he does have a unique story so what does  usually end up happening to people  who are trafficked  well what happens can really vary the  your report just highlighted that some  very few survivors  do escape from their traffickers  and may receive assistance from local  communities from  service providers from ngos police  or others but many continue for years  in situations of severe exploitation  in the context of sexual exploitation  they may be repeatedly raped  beaten enclosed within apartments or  houses  and forced into prostitution others in  the context of labor exploitation  are forced to work for years with little  or no  payments and often subject to very  severe physical abuse  along with the labor exploitation so the  the it is a very serious human rights  violation and one that can continue  over many years and the number of  trafficking victims especially  children has gone up uh in recent years  siobhan what impact  has the pandemic had the pandemic has  had a very serious impact and  this is a concern as we see children  out of school increasingly online  and therefore at risk of traffickers who  work  online who seek to groom and identify  children  and recruit them into situations of  armed conflict  labor exploitation but also of course  online sexual exploitation  and with the collapse of informal  economies in many parts of the world  we see rising unemployment we see  migrant workers being returned home  with nothing to support them and  children may become  a key source of income so we are also  seeing and hearing reports of increases  in child marriage  and this is a very serious concern we  also saw this in the context of the  ebola crisis in west africa  where we saw increases in exploitation  of children and women in particular  who tend to be concentrated in informal  economies  we tend to talk about trafficking in the  context of developing countries but it  does  also exist in the developing world so  what do you see there  it trafficking can happen anywhere so  it may be in the construction sector it  may be in restaurants and tourism  it may be in the context of sexual  exploitation  it's often hidden in plain sight uh so  it it  it's all around us um and we see in  particular  uh workers being exploited in  agriculture  in fruit picking in vegetable picking  in the construction sector and and in  the context of hospitality and  restaurants and  not enough resources are going into  labor inspectorates  uh to supporting trade unions to  supporting worker-led initiatives  a concern also is in the context of the  pandemic that fewer victims are being  identified and this was  reported also in the context of pakistan  but this is happening everywhere as  law enforcement resources are being  diverted  as ngos find it more difficult to  operate we're not seeing as many victims  being identified and referred for  protection  if you could identify one priority one  measure that could be implemented to  implement it to address human  trafficking  in the short term what would that be  i think we really need to see what is  happening in supply chains  in the context of labor exploitation and  in particular  exploitation of children where we see  children  being forced into labor exploitation in  the context of cocoa plantations tobacco  plantations  high numbers of children being  trafficked for the purpose of labor  exploitation  particularly in the west african region  but generating  huge profits for multinational  corporations  and we really need to move beyond  looking at voluntary compliance by the  business sector  and taking seriously what is happening  and identifying victims of trafficking  in their supply chains  we will have to leave it there siobhan  mullally un's special rapporteur on  human trafficking good to have you with  us  thank you
 

